<script>
  import Meta from '../components/Meta.brm';
  import shapes from '../home/shapes.md';
  import effects from '../home/effects.md';
  import mutable from '../home/mutable.md';
  import targets from '../home/targets.md';
  import { raw } from 'brumate/html';

  const RUN_OUTPUT = '<span class="prompt">$ narrac run shapes.nra</span>\narea 27\narea 12';
  const TARGETS = [
    'MacOS-Arm64    Linux-Arm64-musl',
    'MacOS-X86_64   Linux-X86_64-musl',
    'Linux-X86_64   Windows-X86_64',
    'Linux-Arm64    Wasi-Wasm32',
  ].join('\n');
</script>

<Meta
  title="Narra"
  description="Narra is a compiled language with TypeScript-style syntax and Rust-style semantics. It is memory safe without a garbage collector and builds native binaries through C."
  path=""
/>
<main class="home">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <h1>Narra</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        TypeScript-style syntax, Rust-style semantics. Memory safe without a garbage collector, compiled to native binaries through C.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/guide/01-getting-started">Read the guide</a>
        <a class="button" href="/install">Install</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="panel">
      <div class="panel-title">shapes.nra</div>
      {shapes.html}
      <pre class="panel-output">{raw(RUN_OUTPUT)}</pre>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="feature-text">
      <h2>Effects in the signature</h2>
      <p>An exported function says whether it allocates memory or does input and output. The compiler finds the call that needs it and says what to write.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="panel">
      {effects.html}
      <pre class="panel-output diagnostic">Function `describe` uses `alloc` (through `Int.toString`) but its signature does not declare it; write `uses alloc`</pre>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="feature-text">
      <h2>Values, not shared references</h2>
      <p>A value passed as <code>mutable</code> changes in place, with no garbage collector behind it. When another name still holds the old value, the compiler asks for a copy.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="panel">
      {mutable.html}
      <pre class="panel-output diagnostic">damage(mutable hero, ...) may change hero, which saved still uses at line 4; take a copy where it is shared, with copy(...)</pre>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="feature-text">
      <h2>A compiler written in Narra</h2>
      <p>The compiler is Narra source plus one generated C file. A C compiler builds it, and with a cross C compiler such as <code>zig cc</code> it builds for macOS, Linux, Windows and WASI.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="panel">
      {targets.html}
      <pre class="panel-output">{TARGETS}</pre>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <nav class="next-steps" aria-label="Start">
    <a href="/guide/01-getting-started"><strong>Guide</strong><span>Thirteen chapters that build small programs step by step.</span></a>
    <a href="/install"><strong>Install</strong><span>Build the compiler from source and put it on your PATH.</span></a>
    <a href="/spec"><strong>Specification</strong><span>The rules of the language, each with an example.</span></a>
  </nav>
</main>
